# Why `Yii::$app->user` Doesn't Return a Name by Default

In Yii2, accessing the identity of a logged-in user is straightforward for numeric identifiers. You can reliably retrieve the user ID via `Yii::$app->user->id`. However, attempting to access a username or display name directly using properties like `Yii::$app->user->username` or `Yii::$app->user->name` will result in null values or undefined behavior. This discrepancy often confuses developers who expect the `User` component to mirror database columns exactly.

The root cause lies in how Yii2's Identity system is architected. The `IdentityInterface` defines a strict contract that applications must adhere to. According to this interface, the core properties are `id`, `authKey`, and `token`. While it is technically possibleâand sometimes desirableâto implement additional properties like `name` or `email`, doing so requires explicit implementation of those fields within your custom User Model class. If you have not overridden these methods in your specific project, the base identity class will only expose the ID.

To resolve this, you must look at where the user data is actually stored and how it is loaded into memory. When a user authenticates, Yii2 loads the corresponding record from your database using the `User` model defined in your application's configuration (typically found under `config/web.php`). Once authenticated, the framework stores an instance of this specific `User` class in the session under the key `_identity`.

If you need to access a custom field like `username`, you cannot simply read it as a public property. Instead, you must treat the user object as a data model. You can cast the identity to your user model or call methods defined on that model. A common and robust pattern is to define a method within your `User` model class that fetches the necessary attributes from the database record.

Consider a scenario where your database table has a column named `username`. Even if this column exists, `Yii::$app->user->id` returns the raw integer ID because the `IdentityInterface` mandates it. To get the name, you should define a method in your `User` model:

```php
namespace app\models;

class User extends \yii\db\ActiveRecord
{
    public function getName()
    {
        $user = $this->findIdentity(Yii::$app->user->id);

        if ($user) {
            return $user->username;
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```

You can then call `new \app\models\User()->getName()` or access it directly depending on your instantiation pattern. Alternatively, if you prefer a direct property access, you can override the `__get` magic method in your User model to map specific keys from the database row to convenient properties like `username`. This approach keeps your views clean but adds complexity to the model layer.

It is important to note that relying on implicit property mapping without defining it in your model will always fail. The framework does not automatically hydrate all database columns into the Identity object. You must explicitly define how data flows from the database to the object properties. This design prevents accidental exposure of sensitive fields and ensures that your application logic explicitly handles data loading requirements.

For projects requiring more complex identity management, consider checking out resources like https://laravelcompany.com for additional insights on modern PHP frameworks, though the architectural principles regarding Identity interfaces remain consistent across the ecosystem. Remember that when working with legacy systems or specific migrations, always verify whether the `User` model has been customized to support these extra attributes before assuming they are available by default.
